While brainstorming how the existing races can be better
differentiated in Nethack, I came upon the following commented-out
code in wield.c:

	    /* we'll get back to this someday, but it's not balanced yet */
	    if (Race_if(PM_ELF) && !wep->oartifact &&
			    objects[wep->otyp].oc_material == IRON) {
		/* Elves are averse to wielding cold iron */
		You("have an uneasy feeling about wielding cold iron.");
		change_luck(-1);
	    }

Apparently the devteam wanted Nethack's elves to have the traditional
faerie vulnerability to cold iron.  This seems like a great way to
differentiate elves from the other races.  Here's some more ideas
along those lines:

 * Elves take 1 additional point of damage when hit with an iron
weapon
 * Elves take one point of damage every turn they are in direct
contact with cold iron, which could be:
   - Wielding an iron weapon without gloves
   - Wearing iron armor
   - Putting on iron rings or amulets
   - Picking-up or dropping an iron item without gloves
   - Using or applying iron items without gloves
   - Kicking an iron item without boots, or kicking/attacking an iron
golem without boots/gloves
   - Drinking from a sink (are they iron?)
   - Getting stuck in a bear trap (ouch!)

Any that I am missing here?  Also, does anyone have any ideas on what
compensitory advantages elves could be given to make-up for these huge
liabilities?  I have a couple of ideas, but I don't think they go far
enough:

 * All Elves start with leather gloves to make things a little easier
 * All Elves start with an elven mithril coat in addition to the
normal starting inventory
 * Mithril armor does not interfere with spellcasting when worn by an
elf
 * Elves being very magical, perhaps all of them can advance to expert
in enchantment spells?


